title: A fork copies one layer and shares everything below it.
pid: 7a983622-e1be-4ecf-ae55-0069ac90f516
body: |2-
  The clone C of a foreign node N copies N's title, body and the IDs of
  N's children, then subscribes to N and overrides N ('build_fork_clone'
  in 'server/from_text/fork.rs'). N's children are not copied: C
  contains the very nodes N does. So everything below the first layer
  is the author's live notes. When the author changes a grandchild, you
  see the change in place, at once, and nothing tells you.
  Jeff's early design, [[id:81e02908-c814-43ca-9d2f-3d1cac4e6d15][cloning involves recursive subscription]], cloned the
  children too. The shallow fork is what makes forking cheap, and it is
  also why following reaches one layer deep.
